Author: Ivan Kozik <ivan@ludios.org>
Commit: Ivan Kozik <ivan@ludios.org>

    lib: merge worktree-error-recovery; map UnexpectedEof to io::ErrorKind::UnexpectedEof
    
    Model-output: Claude Opus 5
    
    <prompt>
    
    thank you. can you please fix up and finish my merge after I did `git merge --ff worktree-error-recovery`, there's a conflict
    
    </prompt>
    
    <slop>
    
    **The merge is committed.** Only the changelog actually conflicted. The four source files merged textually, so I checked the combined decoder logic by hand rather than trusting it.
    
    **The conflict** was purely additive on both sides of `CHANGELOG_LIB.md`: each branch appended bullets under Fixed and under Added. I kept both sets.
    
    **What needed judgement** was `decode.rs`, which git merged without complaint. The other branch rewrote the decompress loop to verify frame trailers, while mine added an early return when a read fails after output was already produced. Combined, that early return breaks out of the new loop, and the next call re-evaluates the trailer condition, retries the read and surfaces the error. The pending-trailer state survives the break, so a truncated or corrupted frame is still caught on the retry rather than being skipped.
    
    **One deferred piece is now done.** The new `From<Error> for std::io::Error` maps the other branch's `UnexpectedEof` to `io::ErrorKind::UnexpectedEof`, keeping the zeekstd error as the source. That arm could only be written once both branches were on one tree, which is why both sessions left it to whoever merged. I added a test that reads a truncated source through the decoder's `Read` impl and checks the kind. Removing the arm makes it fail with `Other`, which I verified.
    
    47 library tests, 36 without `std`, and the CLI suite all pass. rustfmt is clean, and clippy reports only three warnings whose sites both predate this work.
    
    </slop>
    
